EXPERIMENT 6 SUMMARY
======================================================================
Zero-shot (K=0):  84.19%

K= 1  ( 101 images,  0.15% of pool):  41.30% +/- 2.46   (-42.89 pts vs zero-shot)
K= 2  ( 202 images,  0.30% of pool):  54.87% +/- 1.88   (-29.32 pts vs zero-shot)
K= 4  ( 404 images,  0.59% of pool):  67.21% +/- 0.73   (-16.98 pts vs zero-shot)
K= 8  ( 808 images,  1.19% of pool):  75.69% +/- 0.51   (-8.50 pts vs zero-shot)
K=16  (1616 images,  2.37% of pool):  80.24% +/- 0.37   (-3.95 pts vs zero-shot)

Few-shot does not overtake zero-shot within K <= 16. This is itself a finding: CLIP's pre-trained alignment is stronger than what a probe can learn from this little supervision.

Largest standard deviation across trials: 2.46 pts (at K=1), which is why single-run few-shot numbers are unreliable.

Experiment 6 complete.
